CAT = "rsracTrade"; SEG = "TRD"
Q = [
("When copper is brazed under a nitrogen purge, why is the purge flow kept low?",
 "High flow cools the joint", ["Nitrogen is costly to waste", "Low flow tests the joint", "Nitrogen burns at high flow"],
 "A gentle purge is enough to push air out of the tube so no oxide scale forms. A strong flow cools the joint and can build pressure that blows molten filler out, leaving pinholes."),
("Which filler suits a copper-to-copper brazed joint without flux?",
 "Copper-phosphorus", ["Lead-tin solder", "High-silver alloy only", "Aluminium filler rod"],
 "Copper-phosphorus (BCuP) alloys self-flux on copper. Joining copper to brass or steel needs flux or a silver-bearing alloy with flux."),
("Dry nitrogen for a pressure test comes from a high-pressure cylinder. What goes between the cylinder and the system?",
 "Pressure regulator", ["Check valve only", "Liquid line filter-drier", "Sight glass"],
 "A full nitrogen cylinder is at many times any system's test pressure. A regulator, ideally with a relief valve downstream, limits the test to the maker's value."),
("A new system holds nitrogen at test pressure for 24 hours, but the reading drops slightly overnight. What check comes first?",
 "Correct the reading for temperature", ["Release it and start again", "Charge refrigerant to find out", "Add nitrogen to the start value"],
 "Gas pressure falls as the ambient temperature drops. Compare readings at the same temperature, or correct for it, before chasing a leak."),
("What does a standing vacuum or decay test after evacuation show?",
 "Leaks or leftover moisture", ["The final refrigerant charge", "Compressor valve wear", "Oil level in the crankcase"],
 "After isolating the pump, a rising micron reading that keeps climbing points to a leak; one that rises then levels off points to moisture still boiling off."),
("Why is triple evacuation used on a system that was open to humid air?",
 "Nitrogen carries water out", ["It adds oil to the compressor", "It is faster than one long pull", "It replaces the decay test"],
 "Pulling a vacuum, breaking it with dry nitrogen and pulling again carries moisture out with the nitrogen. A decay test still confirms the result."),
("A blend is charged as liquid into the suction side of a running system. Why is the flow throttled?",
 "So it flashes before the compressor", ["To keep the blend from separating", "To raise the discharge pressure", "To warm the cylinder faster"],
 "Liquid entering the suction line must flash to vapour before it reaches the compressor, or it can damage the valves. A charging valve or a throttled manifold meters it in slowly; the blend was already kept intact by drawing liquid."),
("What does temperature glide describe in a refrigerant blend?",
 "Boiling range at one pressure", ["Pressure drop along the line set", "Rise in discharge temperature", "Drop in oil viscosity"],
 "A zeotropic blend evaporates and condenses over a range of temperatures at one pressure, from the bubble point to the dew point. That range is the glide."),
("Why is polyolester oil kept sealed and added from small containers?",
 "It is hygroscopic", ["It thickens in sunlight", "It turns flammable in air", "It reacts with copper tubing"],
 "POE oil is hygroscopic, so an open container picks up water from the air. Wet oil forms acids in the system."),
("What colour is a refillable recovery cylinder in Canada?",
 "Grey with a yellow top", ["Green with a white band", "Solid red", "Light blue with a black cap"],
 "The federal code of practice describes recovery cylinders as grey with a yellow top or shoulder band, so they are never confused with new refrigerant containers."),
("Why is refrigerant recovered from a system before it is relocated to a new building?",
 "Venting it is prohibited", ["The oil must be replaced", "The coil must be dried", "The charge loses strength"],
 "Releasing halocarbons is prohibited, so a system being moved is pumped down or recovered into an approved container first."),
("What is the purpose of insulating the suction line of a split air conditioner?",
 "Stops condensation and heat gain", ["Keeps the line from vibrating", "Protects it from refrigerant oil", "Holds the line to the wall"],
 "The cold suction line would sweat and drip, and pick up heat that raises superheat. Closed-cell insulation with sealed joints stops both."),
("A condensing unit draws 28 A at full load. Which conductor size question must be checked first?",
 "Its rated ampacity", ["Its insulation colour", "Its length in the reel", "Its bending radius"],
 "Field wiring is sized for the load by ampacity under the Canadian Electrical Code, using the nameplate's minimum circuit ampacity. Colour and reel length do not set size."),
("Which value on a unit nameplate sets the largest overcurrent device allowed?",
 "Maximum overcurrent protection", ["Rated load amps", "Locked rotor amps", "Minimum circuit ampacity"],
 "MOP (maximum overcurrent protection) is the largest fuse or breaker size allowed. Minimum circuit ampacity sizes the conductors."),
("Which sealant should seal a refrigerant line passing through a fire-rated wall?",
 "Listed firestop product", ["Expanding foam from a can", "Duct tape around the pipe", "Silicone caulk only"],
 "Penetrations of fire separations are sealed with a tested, listed firestop system suited to the pipe and insulation. Ordinary foam does not restore the rating."),
("Why are press fittings for refrigeration chosen by refrigerant and pressure rating?",
 "Seals must suit refrigerant and pressure", ["All press fittings are identical", "They are used on drain lines only", "They replace the need for supports"],
 "Refrigeration press fittings carry O-rings and pressure ratings for specific refrigerants and oils. A plumbing press fitting is not rated for refrigerant pressures."),
]
